course: dictionary
lesson: 9421
title: One word, more than one meaning
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one is right. «A noble boy became a page at about seven, a squire at about fourteen, and could be knighted at about twenty-one.» Page, squire and knight are steps on the way to becoming a knight, so a squire is a young man training to be a knight.
explain: Squire can also mean the main landowner in a village or country area. In old English novels, «the village squire» is an important gentleman who owns much of the land. Ask yourself: what meaning fits here? Then try it in the sentence to check.
explain: In UK speech, squire can be a friendly, old-fashioned way for one man to speak to another, as in «All right, squire?». The clues are the friendly tone and the informal, old-fashioned style, so it is not about land or knights at all.
fact: Context means the words and sentences around a word.
fact: Context clues help you choose the right meaning when a word has more than one meaning.
fact: «Squire» means a young man training to be a knight, who served a knight and carried his shield and armour.
fact: «Squire» can also mean the main landowner in a village or country area, or a friendly, old-fashioned way for a man to address another man.
fact: Phrases to notice: «the village squire», «the squire's manor», «page, squire, knight».
exercise: «A noble boy became a page at about seven, a squire at about fourteen, and could be knighted at about twenty-one.» In this text, what is a squire? | A tall castle on a hill | A knight who has won many battles | A small boy who serves at the table | *A young man training to be a knight
exercise: Ella reads «the village squire» in an old novel. Who is the squire? | A horse that pulls the farm carts | A trader who sells apples at the market | A boy who is just seven years old | *The main landowner in the village
exercise: A market trader says, «What can I get you, squire?». What does squire mean here? | A knight ready to fight in battle | The name of the market | A type of armour | *A friendly, old-fashioned way to address a man
test: In the history of knights, which step comes after page and before knight? | *Squire | Monk | Lord | King
test: «The squire's manor» is best understood as: | A heavy cloak worn in winter | *The house and land of the main landowner | A kind of horse used for riding | A ship that sails across the sea
exam: Which meaning of squire fits «He wanted to serve a knight, carry his shield and learn to fight»? | A farm animal | A kind of cheese | *A young man training to be a knight | A market trader
